para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">Despite past reductions, Manitoba&apos;s emissions increased on the whole from 1990-20<trace>24</trace><trace.deleted/>. Emissions for 20<trace>24</trace><trace.deleted/> totalled 21.<trace>4</trace><trace.deleted/> Mt of GHGs<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">8</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bac11a15f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Environment Canada, National Inventory Report: Greenhouse Gas Sources and Sinks in Canada, 1990-2012 (April 2014), Part 3, Annex 11, Table A11-14, p. 27</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bac38b05f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://publications.gc.ca/collections/collection_2014/ec/En81-4-2012-3-eng.pdf</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> and continued to account for nearly 3&percnt; of Canada&apos;s national total emissions.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">9</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bac38b15f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Manitoba, Manitoba&apos;s Report on Climate Change for 2012, Department of Conservation and Water Stewardship (February 2014) at 6</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bac5fc05f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://perma.cc/D7M6-79S9</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> Between 1990 and 20<trace>24</trace><trace.deleted/>, Manitoba&apos;s GHG emissions grew by 1<trace>7</trace><trace.deleted/><trace>.</trace>&percnt;<trace.deleted/><?WGXPPrightHandPageStart sp=";;7" nep=";;9" hyphenated="n" folio="8-7" backFolio="8-8" backBlank="n" releaseNumber="4" releaseDate="2022;12;30" ?><trace.deleted/></para.text></para><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><trace>Moreover, 4 &percnt; of greenhouse gases from fossil fuels are also released through fugitive sources like production, processing or storage of oil and natural gas (includes venting and flaring). 35&percnt; of remaining emissions come from non-energy intensive sources like methane, 27&percnt; come from livestock, manure management and inorganic fertilizer use in the agriculture sector, 6&percnt; from methane from landfills and wastewater treatment in the waste sector and 2&percnt; from other industrial processes that do not use fossil fuels like production of ammonia, lime, and glass.<footnote date.updated="1" date.trace="1"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">Government of Manitoba, &ldquo;Manitoba&apos;s Path to Net Zero&rdquo; (October 2025) at 6, online (pdf): <cite finder="legacy" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref">https://www.manitoba.ca/asset_library/en/netzero/mb-path-to-net-zero.pdf</ref></cite></para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote></trace><trace.deleted/></para.text></para><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">Unlike neighbouring Saskatchewan which has seen a boom in oil and natural gas production and a corresponding jump in GHG emission rates, the Manitoba economy does not have an industrial sector that is predominant in the production of GHG emissions at the national level.</para.text></para><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">Manitoba is unique in that the majority of its GHG emissions come from many dispersed emitters within a wide range of sectors rather than a few large emitters, as is the case with other Canadian jurisdictions.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">19</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bb009405f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Manitoba, Manitoba&apos;s Report on Climate Change for 2012, Department of Conservation and Water Stewardship (February 2014), at p. 10</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bb030505f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://perma.cc/D7M6-79S9</ref></cite> (accessed January 2020).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote><trace> Broken down by economic sector, agriculture accounts for approximately 33&percnt; of GHG emissions, followed by transportation at 31&percnt;, building heat at 13&percnt;, industry (heavy, light manufacturing, construction and forestry) at 11&percnt;, waste at 7&percnt;, and oil and gas at 4&percnt;. Electricity accounts for less than one per cent of Manitoba&apos;s GHG emissions.</trace><footnote date.updated="1" date.trace="1"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">Government of Manitoba, &ldquo;Manitoba&apos;s Path to Net Zero&rdquo; (October 2025) at 7, online (pdf): <cite finder="legacy" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref">https://www.manitoba.ca/asset_library/en/netzero/mb-path-to-net-zero.pdf</ref></cite></para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote><trace.deleted/><footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">20</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bb07e705f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Environment and Climate Change Canada, National Inventory Report 1990-2020: Greenhouse Gas Sources and Sinks in Canada (2022), at Part 3, Annex 11, Table A11-14, p.28</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bb0a5805f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://unfccc.int/documents/461919</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote><trace.deleted/></para.text></para><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">Between 1990 and 202<trace>4</trace><trace.deleted/>, emissions from the transport sector increased from <trace>4.9</trace><trace.deleted/> Mt to <trace.deleted/><trace>6.3</trace> Mt, contributing <trace>29.7</trace><trace.deleted/>&percnt; to the 20<trace>24</trace><trace.deleted/> <?WGXPPrightHandPageStart sp=";;9" nep=";;11" hyphenated="n" folio="8-9" backFolio="8-10" backBlank="n" releaseNumber="4" releaseDate="2022;12;30" ?>provincial total.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">22</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bb141c05f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Environment and Climate Change Canada, National Inventory Report 1990-2020: Greenhouse Gas Sources and Sinks in Canada (2022), at Part 3, Annex 11, Table A11-14, p.28</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bb141c15f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://unfccc.int/documents/461919</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> The majority of these emissions are related to road transportation (<trace>4.5</trace><trace.deleted/> Mt<trace>)</trace><trace.deleted/> with the increase largely attributable to more <trace.deleted/><trace>kilometers</trace> driven and an increase in the total vehicle fleet. An increase in the use of light-duty gasoline trucks (LDGTs) and heavy-duty gasoline trucks (HDGTs) over light-duty gasoline vehicles (LDGVs) continues to reflect a national trend in driver preference and also contributed to the increase in emissions.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">24</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bb18fe05f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Environment and Climate Change Canada, National Inventory Report 1990-2020: Greenhouse Gas Sources and Sinks in Canada (2022), at Part 1, pp. 45-47</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bb18fe15f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://unfccc.int/documents/461919</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote></para.text></para><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">Between 1990 and 202<trace>4</trace><trace.deleted/>, emissions from the agriculture sector increased from 4.<trace>2</trace><trace.deleted/> Mt to 6.<trace>1</trace><trace.deleted/> Mt, accounting for <trace>roughly </trace>29&percnt; of Manitoba&apos;s total GHG emissions in 202<trace>4</trace><trace.deleted/>.<trace.deleted/> Manitoba&apos;s agriculture emissions stand in contrast to the national average of 8.2&percnt; (or 55 Mt) which has remained relatively stable since 2005 levels.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">26</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bb1b6f25f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Environment and Climate Change Canada, National Inventory Report 1990-2020: Greenhouse Gas Sources and Sinks in Canada (2022), at Part 1, Chapter 2.3, Table 2-3, p.38</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bb1de005f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://unfccc.int/documents/461919</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022). Note however that overall, Canada&apos;s emissions within the sector have increased by 34&percnt; between 1990 and 2020. <cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bb1de015f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">National Inventory Report 2022 at Part 1, p. 145</ref></cite>.</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> These increases are largely associated with increases in N<sub>2</sub>O and CH<sub>4</sub> which are attributable to increases in the application of inorganic nitrogen fertilizer in crop production and changes in livestock populations and manure management.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">27</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bb1de025f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Environment and Climate Change Canada, National Inventory Report 1990-2020: Greenhouse Gas Sources and Sinks in Canada (2022), at Part 1, p. 145 et seq.</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bb205105f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://unfccc.int/documents/461919</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote></para.text></para><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><trace>Over 99&percnt; of Manitoba&apos;s electricity currently comes from renewable sources, including hydro and wind. As a result, emissions from the electricity sector remain very low, accounting for only 0.3 per cent of total provincial GHG emissions. Manitoba imports all its refined fossil fuels, while producing crude oil for export, which accounts for much of the province&apos;s GHG emissions from energy.</trace><footnote date.updated="1" date.trace="1"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">5</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">Government of Manitoba, &ldquo;Manitoba&apos;s Path to Net Zero&rdquo; (October 2025) at 17, online (pdf): <cite finder="legacy" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref">https://www.manitoba.ca/asset_library/en/netzero/mb-path-to-net-zero.pdf</ref></cite></para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote><trace.deleted/></para.text></para><para date.updated="1" date.trace="1"><para.text date.updated="0">Manitoba&apos;s transportation sector accounted for 31 per cent (6.6 Mt) of total provincial</para.text></para><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><trace>GHG emissions in 2023. This number is in large part due to the reliance on personal vehicles for day-to-day travel in the province. Agriculture and agri-food account for over 7 per cent of Manitoba&apos;s GDP, employ 34,000 people, and generate about one-third (7.10 Mt) of provincial GHG emissions. These emissions come from energy use in equipment and vehicles, as well as fertilizers, soils, and livestock. Buildings are Manitoba&apos;s third largest source of emissions (2.8 Mt), making up 13&percnt; of provincial GHGs, mostly from heating. Waste accounted for approximately 7&percnt; of Manitoba&apos;s emissions (1.36 Mt) in 2023, mainly from landfill methane (91&percnt;) and wastewater (7&percnt;). Managing waste is costly, energy-intensive, and creates land use challenges.<footnote date.updated="1" date.trace="1"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">6</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">Government of Manitoba, &ldquo;Manitoba&apos;s Path to Net Zero,&rdquo; (October 2025), online (pdf): <cite finder="legacy" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref">https://www.manitoba.ca/asset_library/en/netzero/mb-path-to-net-zero.pdf</ref></cite></para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote></trace><trace.deleted/></para.text></para></section.body></section><section date.revised="0" date.updated="0"><metadata.block metadata.store="yes"><metadata field="classification"><value>ENV.III.2.e Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Approvals, licences and orders&mdash;Air</value></metadata></metadata.block><section.front date.updated="0"><outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0"><label date.updated="0">&sect;</label><designator date.updated="0">8:3</designator><name date.updated="0">Projected Emissions</name></outline.name.block></section.front><section.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">Under the Kyoto Protocol, Manitoba previously committed to achieving the Canadian target of reducing GHG emissions by 6&percnt; below 1990 levels between 2008-2012.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">See <cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bb27a405f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Manitoba, Executive Summary, Meeting Our Commitments: Reducing Emissions in Manitoba and Beyond, at p. 6</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bb27a415f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://www.gov.mb.ca/asset_library/en/beyond_kyoto/executive_summary.pdf</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> This commitment became law in Manitoba in the form of the <ital>Climate Change and Emissions Reductions Act</ital> (&ldquo;the <ital>2008 Climate Change <?WGXPPrightHandPageStart sp=";;11" nep=";;13" hyphenated="n" folio="8-11" backFolio="8-12" backBlank="n" releaseNumber="4" releaseDate="2022;12;30" ?>Act</ital>&rdquo;).<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><ital>The Climate Change and Emissions Reduction Act</ital>, C.C.S.M. c. C135 (&ldquo;<ital>The 2008 Climate Change Act</ital>&rdquo;) (repealed November 8, 2018).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> The Act received Royal Assent and came into force on June 12, 2008, but was repealed by <ital>The Climate and Green Plan Act</ital><footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><ital>The Climate and Green Plan Act</ital>, C.C.S.M., c. C134.</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> which came into force on November 8, 2018 (&ldquo;<ital>The 2018 Climate and Green Plan Act</ital>&rdquo;).</para.text></para><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">The repeal of <ital>The 2008 Climate Change Act</ital> and introduction of the <ital>Made-in-Manitoba Climate and Green Plan</ital> along with the promulgation of <ital>The 2018 Climate and Green Plan Act</ital><footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><ital>The Climate and Green Plan Act</ital>, C.C.S.M., c. C134.</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> came soon after the release of a highly critical report issued by Manitoba&apos;s Office of the Auditor General in October 2017 (&ldquo;Auditor General&apos;s Report&rdquo;).<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">5</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bb2a1505f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Office of the Auditor General, Manitoba, Managing Climate Change (a follow-up to our December 2010 audit) (October 2017), at p.11</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bb2c8605f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://www.oag.mb.ca/audit-reports/report/managing-climate-change-a-follow-up-to-our-december-2010-audit/</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022). For the earlier 2010 report, see <cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bb2c8615f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Office of the Auditor General, Manitoba, Report to the Legislative Assembly&mdash;Performance Audits (December 2010)</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bb2c8625f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://www.oag.mb.ca/audit-reports/report/report-to-the-legislative-assembly-performance-audits/</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> The Auditor General&apos;s Report was an update of a previous 2010 report and covered the period of April 2010 to July 2017. The Report was part of a collaboration involving most provincial legislative audit offices and the Auditor General of Canada and sought to determine the extent to which federal, provincial and territorial governments were meeting commitments to reduce GHG emissions and adapting to climate change.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">6</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bb2ef705f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Office of the Auditor General, Manitoba, Perspectives on Climate Change Action in Canada (March 2018), at p. 3</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bb2ef715f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://www.oag.mb.ca/audit-reports/report/perspectives-on-climate-change-action-in-canada/</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote></para.text></para><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">According to the Auditor General&apos;s Report, although the Department of Sustainable Development was aware by the fall of 2009 that Manitoba was not going to meet its legislated 2012 target, the Department did not update its plan until December 2015.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">7</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bb316805f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Office of the Auditor General, Manitoba, Managing Climate Change (a follow-up to our December 2010 audit) (October 2017), at p.3</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bb316815f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://www.oag.mb.ca/audit-reports/report/managing-climate-change-a-follow-up-to-our-december-2010-audit/</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022). Note that the Department of Sustainable Development is now the Department of Environment, Climate and Parks. For further discussion see this chapter at <cite finder="manual" type="secondaryxref"><ref ref.id="I0bb316825f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" secref="8:8">&sect;8:8</ref></cite>.</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> The Auditor General further criticised the December 2015 Plan as being too &ldquo;high-level&rdquo; and lacking in sufficient detail, analysis (including scientific and economic), inter-departmental monitoring and reporting to comprehensively set and tackle emissions targets.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">8</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bb33d905f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Office of the Auditor General, Manitoba, Managing Climate Change (a follow-up to our December 2010 audit) (October 2017), at pp. 3-4, 18-20</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bb33d915f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://www.oag.mb.ca/audit-reports/report/managing-climate-change-a-follow-up-to-our-december-2010-audit/</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> The Auditor General also found that the Department&apos;s 2011 initiative to create a three-phase provincial plan for adapting to climate change impacts had not progressed across government departments.<foot